<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:media="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Media;assembly=PresentationCore">

    <!--
        What the controls in this window are made of, and not one value of its own. Every
        number, every colour and every thickness below is a name declared in Values.xaml.

        ADR-23, the same rule as the other half: a value invented here would be as invisible
        as one invented in a view, and the guard treats both theme files alike.

        NEITHER HALF LOADS ON ITS OWN, AND THIS ONE FAILS TWICE OVER. It reaches WPF UI's
        keys - DefaultDataGridStyle, DefaultDataGridRowStyle, BodyTextBlockStyle and the rest
        - and it reaches ours in Values.xaml, and StaticResource resolves both WHILE THE FILE
        IS BEING READ. So the merge order in App.xaml is the library's dictionaries, then
        Values.xaml, then this. Anything that loads this file elsewhere has to do the same,
        which is why WpfHost and tools/wpfui-probe both build the stack in that order.

        Split out of Theme.xaml on 2026-08-11 with nothing rewritten - the styles below are
        the ones that were there, in the order they were in. Backlog 156.
    -->

    <!--
        Default look per control type, so a control is consistent without anybody remembering
        to apply a style. The guard checks these too: every Value below points at a name.
    -->
    <!--
        THE THREE ROW STATES LIVE ON THE ROW, AND THAT MOVED HERE FROM THE CELL ON 2026-08-02.
        Read the next four paragraphs before touching either style - this exact question has
        now been got wrong twice, in opposite directions, and both times silently.

        Under the built-in Fluent theme the cell painted an OPAQUE background and covered the
        row, so a Background setter on DataGridRow reached nothing. Three states sat there,
        exactly one appeared, and it only appeared because the same brush was also on the cell.
        Fourteen frames of a service stopping, and the declared #3A3320 never showed once.

        Under WPF UI it is the other way round, and this is read out of their markup rather
        than guessed: DefaultDataGridRowStyle templates its border as
        Background="{TemplateBinding Background}", while DefaultDataGridCellStyle hardcodes
        Background="Transparent" on its own border and never binds the property at all. So a
        Background setter on the CELL is now the dead one.

        THE PARAGRAPH THAT USED TO FOLLOW SAID WPF UI SUPPLIES NO FEEDBACK TO LEAN ON, BECAUSE
        EVERY VisualState IN THEIR TEMPLATES IS EMPTY. The VisualStates are indeed empty and
        that half was right. The conclusion was wrong, and it was wrong in the direction that
        cost the most: their feedback lives in ControlTemplate.Triggers, not in visual states,
        and it was drawing hover and selection all along - in THEIR brush, over ours, with the
        two states sharing one colour so that they could not be told apart. Whoever reads a
        template for this again: read the triggers, not only the states.

        None of it applies to the row any more, because the row no longer uses their template.
        The cell still does.

        RowStyle and CellStyle are set explicitly on the DataGrid in MainWindow.xaml rather
        than left to implicit lookup. Their DefaultDataGridStyle sets both properties itself,
        and an explicit setter on the grid beats an implicit style - so leaving these keyed but
        unreferenced would be a third way to make the same mistake.

        tools/gui-probe/check.ps1 is the only instrument that can tell whether any of this
        reaches a pixel. A property read says #3A3320 either way.
    -->
    <!--
        The grid itself, and it exists for one setter that is not optional.

        DataGridTextColumn TRANSFERS FontSize from the grid onto every TextBlock it generates,
        as a LOCAL value - and a local value beats a default style. So the implicit TextBlock
        style below, which carries our size, loses inside every cell of this list. Their
        DefaultDataGridStyle sets FontSize to their own DefaultDataGridFontSize, and the first
        build after the migration came out with rows a third taller and the PID column clipped
        to two digits, because the wider text pushed the automatic columns past the viewport.

        Neither symptom looked like a font size, and that is why this comment is here.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ListGrid" TargetType="DataGrid" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultDataGridStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE ROW OWNS ITS OWN TEMPLATE SINCE 2026-08-05, AND THAT IS THE FIX FOR A FAMILY OF
        FAULTS RATHER THAN FOR ONE OF THEM. Read this before moving a state back into a style
        trigger, because that is the shape that failed.

        What was wrong: the four states were setters on DataGridRow.Background inside this
        style. Their template feeds that property to its border through a TemplateBinding, so
        it looks like it should arrive - and it never did. Their template also carries a
        trigger on ItemsControl.AlternationIndex = 0 that sets the same border's Background
        from the grid's RowBackground, and AlternationIndex is zero for every row while
        AlternationCount is zero, so it is on for every row all the time. Inside a template,
        a trigger's setter outranks a TemplateBinding on the same element. Our colour lost that
        contest on every row, every frame, in silence.

        This is the FIFTH time in this product that XAML bound correctly and painted something
        else, after the empty list from internal members, column headers outside the visual
        tree, the cell painting over the row under the built-in theme, and the white search
        box. Rule 7 of the project notes says a problem that keeps coming back is a wrong
        primitive rather than a missing tweak. The wrong primitive was borrowing a template and
        arguing with it from outside.

        WHAT WE GIVE UP BY OWNING IT: their template's future improvements, and about forty
        lines to maintain. WHAT WE GET: four states that are template triggers, which is the
        one place nothing can outrank, plus a zebra stripe that becomes possible again - the
        two AlternationIndex triggers below are kept and come FIRST, so setting
        AlternatingRowBackground on the grid would stripe the list without touching any state.
        Backlog 123 asks for exactly that and could not have it while the states were dying.

        STILL NOT PROVEN AT THE PIXEL. This explains a measurement rather than replacing one.
        tools/gui-probe/check.ps1 is the instrument, and it needs a person.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ListRow" TargetType="DataGridRow" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultDataGridRowStyle}">
        <!--
            Margin and padding pulled back to nothing, against their 0,2 and 4. The owner chose
            to keep this list's density on 2026-08-02: their spacing costs about 17% of the
            rows on screen, measured twice, and this window exists to show 810 services.
        -->
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginNone}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource MarginNone}" />

        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="DataGridRow">
                    <Border x:Name="RowSurface"
                            Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                            SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
                        <Grid>
                            <SelectiveScrollingGrid>
                                <SelectiveScrollingGrid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                                </SelectiveScrollingGrid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                <SelectiveScrollingGrid.RowDefinitions>
                                    <RowDefinition Height="*" />
                                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                                </SelectiveScrollingGrid.RowDefinitions>

                                <DataGridCellsPresenter
                                    Grid.Column="1"
                                    ItemsPanel="{TemplateBinding ItemsPanel}"
                                    SnapsToDevicePixels="{TemplateBinding SnapsToDevicePixels}" />

                                <DataGridDetailsPresenter
                                    Grid.Column="1"
                                    Grid.Row="1"
                                    Visibility="{TemplateBinding DetailsVisibility}"
                                    SelectiveScrollingGrid.SelectiveScrollingOrientation="Both" />

                                <!--
                                    The grid decides whether row headers show, through its own
                                    converter. Theirs hardcodes Visible, which is a decision
                                    taken away from HeadersVisibility on the grid.
                                -->
                                <DataGridRowHeader
                                    Grid.Column="0"
                                    Grid.Row="0"
                                    Grid.RowSpan="2"
                                    SelectiveScrollingGrid.SelectiveScrollingOrientation="Vertical"
                                    Visibility="{Binding HeadersVisibility,
                                        ConverterParameter={x:Static DataGridHeadersVisibility.Row},
                                        Converter={x:Static DataGrid.HeadersVisibilityConverter},
                                        RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type DataGrid}}}" />
                            </SelectiveScrollingGrid>

                            <!--
                                A sibling rather than a border on the row, so it costs no
                                layout - an empty Border asks for two pixels and the cells
                                beside it ask for more, so the row height is unchanged. Not
                                hit testable, or the ring would swallow clicks on its own row.
                            -->
                            <Border x:Name="FocusOutline"
                                    BorderBrush="{StaticResource FocusRing}"
                                    BorderThickness="{StaticResource FocusRingThickness}"
                                    Visibility="Collapsed"
                                    IsHitTestVisible="False"
                                    SnapsToDevicePixels="True" />
                        </Grid>
                    </Border>

                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <!--
                            The grid's own two, kept and kept FIRST. They are inert while the
                            grid sets neither brush, and they are what a zebra stripe would
                            come back through - underneath the states rather than over them,
                            which is the arrangement backlog 123 needs.
                        -->
                        <Trigger Property="ItemsControl.AlternationIndex" Value="0">
                            <Setter TargetName="RowSurface"
                                    Property="Background"
                                    Value="{Binding RowBackground,
                                        RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type DataGrid}}}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <Trigger Property="ItemsControl.AlternationIndex" Value="1">
                            <Setter TargetName="RowSurface"
                                    Property="Background"
                                    Value="{Binding AlternatingRowBackground,
                                        RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type DataGrid}}}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <!--
                            First of the three colours, so that pointing at a row or selecting
                            it still wins. A person's own hand beats a notification about
                            something that already happened.
                        -->
                        <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding RecentlyChanged}" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="RowSurface"
                                    Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceChanged}" />
                        </DataTrigger>

                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="RowSurface"
                                    Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHover}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <!--
                            LAST of the three, so selection beats the pointer, and that order is
                            measured rather than assumed. With the pointer winning, a row went
                            grey the instant it was clicked and stayed grey for as long as the
                            hand did not move - so the one row the person had just chosen was
                            the one row that did not look chosen.

                            Foreground goes on the row rather than the border, because it has
                            to reach the text inside the cells and it travels by inheritance.
                        -->
                        <Trigger Property="IsSelected" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="RowSurface"
                                    Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceSelected}" />
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <!--
                            Focus is not one of the three and does not compete with them: it
                            shows an outline and changes no fill, so a row can be selected and
                            focused and still look like both.

                            IsKeyboardFocusWithin rather than IsFocused, because the keyboard
                            lands on a CELL inside the row rather than on the row. Read out of
                            their styles, which set KeyboardNavigation.IsTabStop to False on both
                            the row and the cell, plus how a DataGrid moves focus when the current
                            cell changes. NOT MEASURED at the pixel - that needs an active window,
                            which a test process does not have. Backlog 131.
                        -->
                        <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocusWithin" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="FocusOutline" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        The cell carries spacing and nothing else. Any Background set here is discarded by
        their template - see the block above.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ListCell" TargetType="DataGridCell" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultDataGridCellStyle}">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadListCell}" />
        <!--
            Against their MinHeight of 32. Row height is decided by the padding above, which is
            the same decision as the margins on the row: keep the density this list had.
        -->
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="{StaticResource SizeNone}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        WHAT EVERY CELL IN THE LIST IS MADE OF, AND THE TRIMMING IS THE POINT.

        Without it a cell too narrow for its text cuts it wherever the pixels run out, halfway
        through a character, with nothing to say it did - which reads as a rendering fault
        rather than as "this does not fit". Measured on the real window: a display name wanted
        476 pixels in a column of 365, and at 1039 pixels of window there is no width at which
        the longest of them fits. Something has to give and the person has to be able to see
        that it did.

        The tooltip is the other half of the same rule. Text that gives way has to be gettable,
        and a person who wants the whole of it should not have to widen the window and guess.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CellText" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource BodyTextBlockStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
        <Setter Property="TextTrimming" Value="CharacterEllipsis" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{Binding RelativeSource={RelativeSource Self}, Path=Text}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        Numbers line up on their last digit, text on its first. Complaint 3 of the eleven, and
        it is how a column of process ids becomes comparable at a glance instead of being read
        one row at a time.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CellNumber" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource CellText}">
        <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Right" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        The heading, so it stops looking like data. Complaint 4 of the eleven.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ColumnHeading" TargetType="DataGridColumnHeader" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultDataGridColumnHeaderStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        A heading over numbers sits where the numbers do, or the column reads as two columns.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ColumnHeadingNumber" TargetType="DataGridColumnHeader" BasedOn="{StaticResource ColumnHeading}">
        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Right" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE MARK BESIDE A MEANING, AND FILLED VERSUS HOLLOW IS NOT DECORATION.

        Filled means the entry is doing something - running, or on its way somewhere. Hollow
        means it is not. Colour separates within each pair, so the shape carries the coarse
        answer and the colour the fine one. WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.1 says colour may not be the only
        visual means of conveying information, and the word beside the mark is the third
        channel and always present.

        The trigger compares against a CODE from the view model, never against the word. The
        word is translated; the code is not, and a trigger matching on "Running" would quietly
        stop matching the day a second language file appears - the row would just lose its
        colour, with nothing anywhere saying why. This project has had four of those.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="StatusMark" TargetType="Ellipse">
        <Setter Property="Width" Value="{StaticResource ShapeMark}" />
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="{StaticResource ShapeMark}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginAfterShape}" />
        <Setter Property="StrokeThickness" Value="{StaticResource ShapeOutline}" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}" />
        <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUnknown}" />

        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StatusShape}" Value="shape.running">
                <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRunning}" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRunning}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StatusShape}" Value="shape.transit">
                <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{StaticResource MeaningWarning}" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningWarning}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StatusShape}" Value="shape.stopped">
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StatusShape}" Value="shape.paused">
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningWarning}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <!--
                Explicit rather than left to the style's default, which drew it identically to a
                stopped entry. Backlog 149 - the mark now differs in shape, not in a shade of grey
                nobody can separate.
            -->
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StatusShape}" Value="shape.unknown">
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUnknown}" />
                <Setter Property="StrokeDashArray" Value="{StaticResource ShapeUnreadableOutline}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        The start type's mark, and it is Hidden rather than Collapsed for the ordinary case.
        Collapsed would give the space back and every word in the column would sit at a
        different place depending on whether its entry had something to report, which is a
        ragged column pretending to be information.

        Waiting on a trigger deliberately gets NO mark. It is not a fault - it is why four of
        the ten entries that read as "automatic and not running" on a real machine were fine.
        It is said in words, next to the type, where it explains rather than accuses.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="StartMark" TargetType="Ellipse" BasedOn="{StaticResource StatusMark}">
        <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Hidden" />
        <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUnknown}" />

        <Style.Triggers>
            <!--
                Each mark says what it means, backlog 149. Colour was the only carrier until
                2026-08-10 and docs/11 forbids exactly that - the owner reported seeing dots that
                were sometimes empty and sometimes red with no way to find out why.
            -->
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.missing">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUntrusted}" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUntrusted}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.missing}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.disabled">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.disabled}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.unknown">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUnknown}" />
                <Setter Property="StrokeDashArray" Value="{StaticResource ShapeUnreadableOutline}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.unknown}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        BasedOn by NAME on WPF UI's own styles, not on {x:Type TextBlock}.

        The type form worked while the base was the built-in Fluent theme, and would now be
        ambiguous: this dictionary is merged after theirs and is itself keyed by the same type,
        so which style it resolves to depends on lookup order rather than on intent. Their keys
        are stable, documented by their own markup - DataGrid.xaml bases four styles on exactly
        these names - and they say what is meant.

        CONSEQUENCE TO KNOW: this file no longer loads on its own. It resolves keys that only
        exist once ControlsDictionary is merged, which is why tools/wpfui-probe loads it after
        theirs. That is a real dependency and it is the shape of the decision the owner made.

        Every one of the three below keeps OUR size setter on top of their template. That is
        the density decision of 2026-08-02 in the two places it is spelled out.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource BodyTextBlockStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="HeadingText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeading}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="FixedText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFaceFixed}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="SubduedText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE COUNT, WHICH MOVED OUT FROM UNDER THE LIST AND STOPPED BEING SUBDUED. Complaint 8 of
        the eleven says it was the least visible thing on the screen and is the main answer to
        every keystroke - small, grey and at the bottom is three ways of saying "ignore me".

        SEMIBOLD WHEN THE LIST IS NARROWED, AND THAT IS COMPLAINT 7 RATHER THAN DECORATION. That
        one says you cannot see THAT a filter is on. The words already say it - "108 of 810"
        against "810" - but a weight change is seen without reading, which is the difference
        between a signal and a fact you have to go and check.

        TextSizeHeader rather than TextSizeHeading: 14 is the floor for semibold text in the
        guidance TypeScaleGuards holds, and this line is semibold half the time.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CountLine" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightNormal}" />

        <!--
            Missing until 2026-08-10, and the count was black on a dark window. A regression from
            slice 5, where it "stopped being grey" - the brush was removed rather than replaced,
            so this style declared type and no colour and inherited one nobody chose. Backlog 145.
        -->
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Says.Narrowed}" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        The sentence in the middle of an empty list. Larger than the rows, because it is the only
        thing on that half of the screen and somebody is looking at it wondering what happened.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="EmptyStateText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeading}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        What the result has to admit about itself, and what is wrong with the question. Two
        styles rather than one, because they answer different questions and a person glancing
        at the window has to be able to tell "your query is broken" from "your query worked and
        the answer has holes in it" without reading either sentence.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="NoticeText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource MeaningWarning}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="ProblemText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRejected}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        The one field. Its padding is named rather than left to the framework because the hint
        sitting behind it has to line up with the text a person types, and the only way to be
        sure of that is for both to come from this line.

        BasedOn is the whole reason this comment is long. Without it a default style here does
        not ADD to the base one, it REPLACES it - so the field came out white, with grey hint
        text, on a dark window. It shipped that way from S6a until 2026-08-02, through a
        screenshot review that had the field on screen.

        Found by accident, while photographing this window to compare it against a control
        library. That is the fourth time in this project that XAML has bound correctly and
        painted something other than what it promised, after the empty list from internal view
        models, the column headers outside the visual tree, and the dead DataGridRow background.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="TextBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultTextBoxStyle}">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadCell}" />
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="CheckBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultCheckBoxStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
